A diode-connected nMOS transistor always operates in saturation region whenever \(V_{GS}&gt;V_T\) The drain current in saturation is given by \[I_D=K(V_{GS}-V_T)^2\] Given, \(V_T=1\,\text{V}\) and When \(V_{GS}=2\,\text{V}\) , \(I_D=1\,\text{mA}\). Thus, \[\begin{aligned}
1\,\text{mA}
&amp;=K(2-1)^2 \\
\end{aligned}
\implies K=1\,\text{mA/V}^2\] Now for \(V_{GS}=3\,\text{V}\) \[I_D
=K(3-1)^2 
=1\times (2)^2 
=4\,\text{mA}\] \[\boxed{\text{(D)}\quad 4\,\text{mA}}\]

Given, \(I_{C}=1\,\text{mA},
V_{T}=25\,\text{mV} \text{ and } \beta = 100\) For a BJT operating in the active region, \[g_{m}=\frac{I_{C}}{V_{T}}\] \[g_{m}
=\frac{1\,\text{mA}}{25\,\text{mV}}
=40\,\text{mA/V}\] The transconductance and input resistance are related by \[g_{m}=\frac{\beta}{r_{\pi}}\implies 
r_{\pi}=\frac{\beta}{g_{m}}\] \[r_{\pi}
=\frac{100}{40\times10^{-3}}
=2500\,\Omega
=2.5\,\text{k}\Omega\] \[\boxed{\text{(D)\quad $g_m = 40mA/V$ and $r_\pi = 2.5k\Omega$}}\]

Given polynomial: \[P(s) = s^5 + s^4 + 2s^3 + 2s^2 + 3s + 15\] \[\begin{array}{c|ccc}
s^5 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 \\
s^4 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 15 \\
s^3 &amp; \epsilon &amp; -12  \\ s^2 &amp; \dfrac{2\epsilon + 12}{\epsilon} \approx \dfrac{12}{\epsilon}\,(+\text{ve}) &amp; 15 \\ s^1 &amp; \dfrac{\left[\frac{12}{\epsilon}(-12)\right] - 15\epsilon}{\frac{12}{\epsilon}} \approx -12\,(-\text{ve}) &amp; &amp; \\
s^0 &amp; 15 &amp; &amp;
\end{array}\] Since a first-element row value becomes zero (\(s^3\) row), it is replaced by a small positive number \(\epsilon \to 0^+\). As there are 2 sign changes in the first column, 2 roots lie in the right half of the \(s\)-plane. \[\boxed{(B) \ \ 2}\]

Given, \[\text{Poles at }0.1~\text{Hz},~1~\text{Hz},~80~\text{Hz}
\quad
and
\quad
\text{Zeros at }5~\text{Hz},~100~\text{Hz},~200~\text{Hz}\] The phase contribution of a pole or zero depends on the relation between operating frequency and break frequency. At frequency much higher than break frequency, \[\text{Pole contributes }-90^\circ\] \[\text{Zero contributes }+90^\circ\] At frequency much lower than break frequency, \[\text{Phase contribution} \approx 0^\circ\] Now, the response is required at \[f=20~\text{Hz}\] <strong>Phase contribution of poles</strong><br/>\[\begin{aligned}
0.1~\text{Hz} &amp;\ll 20~\text{Hz}
&amp;&amp;\Rightarrow -90^\circ
\\
1~\text{Hz} &amp;\ll 20~\text{Hz}
&amp;&amp;\Rightarrow -90^\circ
\\
80~\text{Hz} &amp;&gt; 20~\text{Hz}
&amp;&amp;\Rightarrow 0^\circ
\end{aligned}\] Hence, \[\phi_p=-180^\circ\] <strong>Phase contribution of zeros</strong><br/>\[\begin{aligned}
5~\text{Hz} &amp;&lt; 20~\text{Hz}
&amp;&amp;\Rightarrow +90^\circ
\\
100~\text{Hz} &amp;&gt; 20~\text{Hz}
&amp;&amp;\Rightarrow 0^\circ
\\
200~\text{Hz} &amp;&gt; 20~\text{Hz}
&amp;&amp;\Rightarrow 0^\circ
\end{aligned}\] Hence, \[\phi_z=+90^\circ\] Therefore, total phase is \[\phi=\phi_z+\phi_p\] \[\phi=(+90^\circ)+(-180^\circ)\] \[\phi=-90^\circ\] \[\boxed{\text{(A) }-90^\circ}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Pole contributes approximately \(-90^\circ\) at frequencies much higher than its break frequency.</li><li>Zero contributes approximately \(+90^\circ\) at frequencies much higher than its break frequency.</li><li>At frequencies much lower than break frequency, phase contribution is approximately \(0^\circ\).</li></ul></div>

